one of the women that you spoke to about  to contribute to the book was theresa  may  um how do you reflect or how did you  both reflect on  how she was treated in her role  one of the contributors was theresa may  that's right in the book to  eight women who either had or are  presidents or prime ministers plus  hillary clinton of course  uh theresa may's experience actually  echoed that of a number of the other  women  she talked about a differential focus on  appearance she talked about  the tight rope that women have to walk  between  strength and empathy she talked to about  disproportionate media attention  in her emotional reactions constant  questioning of her press people about  whether she was going to be moved to  tears whether she was going to cry  and of course when she gave her final  press conference that was largely  reported  as if she had been sobbing whereas if  you watch the footage yourself that's  simply not true  so she did have a number of experiences  in common with other women leaders  she i think as far as the book is  concerned she said  if a male leader cries he's seen as  patriotic  but if a woman cries she's seen as over  emotional that's a pretty narrow  tightrope for women to walk in power  isn't it  it's an extraordinary narrow tightrope  and we know  it exists not just because women like  theresa may  speak of it but because there's been  academic research  into this tightrope there have been  universities that have done  things like got randomised groups of  voters  presented one group with a male  candidate another group with a female  candidate  got them to deliver exactly the same  script  and then monitored the reactions and we  know from that kind of research work  that unless women weave a very narrow  path  between being tough enough to lead but  still looking at  nurturing and caring unless they get  that right  then they can be very much rejected by  voters and particularly a woman  who is seen not to be caring or  empathetic  who seem to be a very tough leader will  get a very visceral  and adverse reaction from voters i have  a friend  who was a man and he is a scientist he  gave  a conference speech and he decided to  transition into being a woman changed  his name  some years later about four years later  gave him a speech  again within the same group and the  feedback for  him then as a woman was your brother was  better than you were  how surprised are you with that doesn't  that say  absolutely everything and we do know  from other studies that this is in fact  a very common experience  there was an american study where a  course was  taught wholly online and people never  actually met  the teacher and so they got a male  teacher  to say he was male to the students a  female teacher to say she was female  but then in different classes they  swapped up the roles  and when students believed that they  were being taught by a male  teacher they gave better evaluations  even though in truth it was exactly the  same person who was teaching them  both in the guys as a man and as a woman  and so  yes unfortunately we do still evaluate  so much through the prism of gender and  the book is an attempt to get us to  think about that  to think about how global it is i'm a  co-author  an african friend of mine dr ngozia  conjury wheeler wrote this book with me  and we're asking people in all cultures  and contexts to think about  how we still don't give women leaders an  equal go  and what we can do to change that  do we expect women leaders to react  differently  to global pandemics like  coronavirus and covet 19.  i don't know if we expect them to but  there is some  evidence coming out now that women  leaders have generally done  better in the grips of the pandemic this  is actually uk  academic research from development  economists and the conclusion that they  come to is that women leaders are  more risk averse than male leaders  perhaps they intuitively know that women  do  still pay a greater price for errors and  as a result  the women leaders of countries tended to  go for  quicker restrictions to control the  spread of the virus  and that has been a good strategy to  save lives and to minimise illness  i do want to talk to you of course about  tony abbott  he's a hot topic here in the united  kingdom at the moment as i'm sure you  know you were one of the first people to  call him out  as a misogynist why do you think he was  and do you think he still is  well i said everything i needed to say  about  that back in october 2012 and that  speech is still doing the rounds all of  these years later  and i stand by every word of it but i  don't think  that i need to add to it so i put my  views on the record  of course it's not for me to work out  who should be the  uk trade envoy or specialist this is a  decision for the uk government  and ultimately for the views of the  people of britain so i will leave all of  that to you  well let's have a quick listen let's  remind our viewers and for those who  haven't heard what you said here's a  quick  snippet of what you said at the time i  will  not be lectured about sexism and  misogyny by this man i will not  and the government will not be elected  about sexism and misogyny  by this man not now not ever  and he also stood in front of placards  that described you as a witch and  another man's  [ __ ] i know that you say that you don't  want to talk about it but do not feel  slightly that you that you have a voice  here and that for women  who potentially find themselves in a  position where they  are undermined by men that somebody of  your experience  to have a word or two about what might  happen would be valuable to them  i certainly do think that i've got a  voice and having had those experiences  that i spoke about in 2012  it's motivated me to go on and to try  and  get the message out about women about  gender equality  that's what brings me regularly to the  united kingdom with king's college  london i established and it's still  thriving a global  institute for women's leadership getting  through fantastic research work  even in the middle of lockdowns and  other disruptions  and it's what's motivated me with ngosi  to write this book  i can't personally mentor or speak to  every woman  who wants to learn from my experiences  but what i can do  is write them down and make them  available and that's what the book's  about  i do wish that i'd had a book like this  when i was starting out because i think  forewarned is forearmed  and if you can think for yourself how  you will deal with those gendered  moments  when unfortunately they inevitably come  then  it will be an easier time to get through  than just  encountering them as i did and trying to  work out what to do in the moment  yeah but you are a strong woman as am i  you know we don't tolerate  misogyny and younger women who were  trying to bring up the greasy pearl with  us are looking to women like us to  support them  when you call out somebody as a  misogynist is it appropriate that he  potentially then  is a trade envoy for a country the other  side  of the world and the health secretary of  that country saying  doesn't matter what he said previously  because he is good at trade  how does that make you feel infuriated  me what about you  [Laughter]  uh well i've been through too many of  these kinds of experiences  to end up being infuriated uh  look this is a decision for the uk and  you know i'm not a citizen of the uk i  was of course born there  but i'm not a citizen of the uk so it's  for your democratic politics to make a  decision  i think my views are clear they're well  known  i don't need to add to them they're no  mystery to people  but you stand by what you said in that  speech some time ago that  tony abbott is a misogynist as far as  you're concerned  i certainly stand by every word of that  speech  i doubt that i'll ever be able to put  it better in my life than the things  that i said  that day on misogyny and so i'll let  that speech speak for  itself okay and your new book is out now  is it  yes it is in the united kingdom now
 

